<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>cn.com.keelbase</groupId>
    <artifactId>keelbase4j</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.1</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>keelbase4j-core</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>KeelBase4J Core</name>
  <description>
    The embeddable runtime core: everything the runtime is, except the decision to be an application.
    An application that already exists — a host platform, say — puts this jar on its classpath and gets
    the trust loop, the governance surface and the security filter chain through one auto-configuration;
    keelbase4j-runtime is the thin application that does the same thing for a standalone deployment.
    What is deliberately absent here is the application's half: no server configuration, no datasource
    of its own, no context path (ADR-0017 D4/D5).
  </description>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>cn.com.keelbase</groupId>
      <artifactId>keelbase4j-protocol</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webmvc</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Authentication lives at the request entry (ADR-0004 D3). The protocol library must stay free
      of this: see the protocol module's pom.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      The core's own schema, and the runner that applies it. The core is handed a database by whoever
      deploys it (ADR-0017 D4), so it cannot leave its tables to a ddl-auto it does not control — a host
      validating its schema refuses to start when a mapped entity has no table, which is the failure the
      migrations exist to prevent. Carrying the runner rather than the SQL files alone is deliberate: an
      application that adds this jar should not also have to be told to apply what is inside it. Boot 4
      has a starter for this; flyway-core alone is no longer enough.

      —— 核心自己的 schema，以及应用它的运行器。核心的数据库由部署方交给它（ADR-0017 D4），所以它不能把
      自己的表交给一个自己控制不了的 ddl-auto —— 一个会校验 schema 的宿主，在某个被映射的实体没有表时
      **拒绝启动**，而迁移正是为了避免那种失败。**带运行器而不是只带 SQL 文件是有意的**：加了这个 jar 的
      应用，不该还要被告知去应用它里面的东西。Boot 4 为此有 starter；只有 flyway-core 已经不够了。
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-flyway</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      MySQL support for Flyway, which is a separate artefact since Flyway 10 and is *not* optional in
      practice: `flyway-core` alone recognises H2 and refuses every other database, and the refusal
      arrives as `FlywayException: Unsupported Database: MySQL 8.0` while the row that owns the
      migration is being created — so a host on MySQL does not start at all, at a point that reads
      like a schema problem and is not. Measured, not anticipated: the first real host run died
      exactly there.

      It sits beside the migrations rather than in the host because this module already ships
      dialect-specific migration files: a jar that carries `db/keelbase-migration/mysql/` and cannot
      apply it is half a feature, and leaving the dialect to the deployment turns a startup
      prerequisite into an easy-to-miss step that fails with a message that names neither the missing
      artefact nor the fix. The cost is that every deployment now carries MySQL support whether or
      not it is a MySQL deployment — a dialect module is inert unless Flyway meets that database, so
      the cost is classpath weight, and a PostgreSQL host would add its own alongside.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
      <artifactId>flyway-mysql</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      PostgreSQL, for the same reason and by the same rule the MySQL module above is here: this
      module ships `db/keelbase-migration/postgresql/`, and a jar that carries a dialect's migrations
      and cannot apply them is half a feature. The host line's decision to run on PostgreSQL is what
      made it concrete (JV-36); the comment above already noted that a PostgreSQL host would have to
      add its own alongside, and shipping it here is cheaper than making every such host remember to.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
      <artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      Test scope only, and that is the point of the split: the core is given a database by whoever
      deploys it, so the embedded one is not a dependency of what this module ships (ADR-0017 D4).
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
      <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      Test scope too, and for the same reason. The PostgreSQL DDL is a byte-identical copy of the h2
      one, which is exactly the kind of claim that should be checked by running rather than by reading:
      the two dialects agree on the types here, but `validate` is also what catches a mapping that has
      drifted, and it can only catch it where that database actually is. A deployment brings its own
      driver; this one exists so the check is a run.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
      <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
</project>
